            {
                id history-regex
                title "Choose and integrate a basic-regex engine for history search"
                deps (history-translate)
                log (
                    {
                        at 2026-08-05T08:19:37.774689188Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "Two facts from src/search.c:118-163 that narrow the engine choice, both missing from the comment on `bre_match`:\n\n1. The regex leg is a FALLBACK, not the primary path. `el_match` opens with `if (wcsstr(str, pat) != 0) return 1;` — a plain wide substring test — and only reaches `regcomp`/`regexec` when the literal substring is absent. Interactive history search is overwhelmingly literal, so engine choice governs a cold path, not a hot one. A heavier or slower engine is affordable here.\n\n2. Matching happens on ENCODED BYTES, not code points. Both operands go through `ct_encode_string(..., &conv)` before `regcomp`/`regexec`, so the C matches in the locale's multibyte encoding. A byte-oriented engine is therefore the faithful choice; a `char`-oriented one would diverge on any multibyte pattern. This also means bracket expressions and `.` operate on bytes, so `.` matches one byte of a UTF-8 sequence, not one character.\n\nFailure semantics: `regcomp` returning non-zero yields `rv = 0` — an unparseable pattern is simply \"no match\", never an error to the caller. cflags is 0, so POSIX BRE with no REG_NEWLINE and no REG_ICASE.\n\nAlso confirmed and NOT a defect: `pat` and `str` are encoded through the same static `conv` buffer, so the second `ct_encode_string` overwrites the first. This is safe only because POSIX `regcomp` copies the pattern rather than retaining the pointer. Worth an errata note as a latent trap for any port that swaps in an engine holding a borrow on the pattern."
                    }
                )
            }
        )
    }

