            {
                id abi-headers
                title "Decide whether the port ships histedit.h, editline/readline.h and the man page"
                log (
                    {
                        at 2026-08-05T08:51:34.552190783Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "Raised by errata-reconcile, which could not adjudicate two register entries without it.\n\nERR-terminal-64 (doc/editline.3 lists SIGSTOP among the signals libedit handles, which it does not) and most of ERR-core-api-34 (annotation errors in histedit.h) both carry a \"fix the documentation\" disposition. The port ships no histedit.h and nobody has touched doc/editline.3, so neither can be resolved or honestly called reproduced. They sit at open/partial until this is settled.\n\nThe question is not cosmetic. A drop-in replacement is consumed at two layers:\n\n- Link time, which is the .so and its exported symbols. That part is decided and being built.\n- COMPILE time, which is the headers. A consumer that does `#include <histedit.h>` and builds against us needs a header from somewhere. If we do not ship one, we are drop-in only for already-compiled binaries, and any consumer rebuilding from source still needs libedit installed — which rather defeats the purpose for a distribution that wants to replace it outright.\n\nShipping headers pulls in consequences worth deciding deliberately rather than discovering:\n\n- The C's headers carry the annotation defects the register already logs. Shipping them verbatim reproduces those defects at compile time for every consumer; fixing them diverges from the artifact we are replacing. That is the same reproduce-versus-fix fork conformance-policy governs, arriving in a place the policy does not obviously reach, because a header is not runtime behaviour.\n- histedit.h declares el_set/el_get as variadic. Our ABI is fixed-arity per the varargs decision, which is correct on x86-64 SysV and aarch64 AAPCS and wrong on aarch64 Apple. A header we ship is where that would have to be stated.\n- editline/readline.h has the completion_matches const mismatch ERR-readline-53 records, which is only observable to a compiling consumer — so it is a header-only defect that shipping would either propagate or fix.\n\nNot blocking anything today. Worth settling before the register is called reconciled, since two entries are parked on it."
                    }
                )
            }

            {
                id abi-missing-exports
                title "Export the nine symbols we are missing, eight of which deployed consumers already reach"
                log (
                    {
                        at 2026-08-05T10:57:51.013898095Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "Measured by conformance/abi-shape.sh, not inferred. The port exports 198 dynamic symbols against the oracle's 227: 29 missing, ZERO extra.\n\nTwenty of the 29 are the vis family and are missing CORRECTLY. Debian's libedit.so.2 has NEEDED libbsd.so.0 and imports strvis@LIBBSD_0.0, strunvis@LIBBSD_0.0 and vis@LIBBSD_0.0 while exporting none of them; the port exports and imports none, so it matches the deployment target. The oracle exports all twenty only because libbsd-dev is absent on this host, so configure answered no to HAVE_VIS/HAVE_UNVIS and src/Makefile.am compiled vis.c and unvis.c in. That is a property of the oracle's build configuration and not a gap. abi-shape.sh already splits the missing set so these never masquerade as one. Do NOT export them — see the interposition hazard on abi-surface.\n\nThe nine that are real:\n\n    ct_decode_string\n    ct_encode_string\n    fn_complete\n    fn_complete2\n    fn_display_match_list\n    fn_filename_completion_function\n    fn_tilde_expand\n    reallocarr\n    wcsdup\n\nEIGHT of those nine — all but wcsdup — are exported by Debian's libedit.so.2 as well. So a consumer deployed today reaches them, and linking against us gives an unresolved symbol. That is the whole drop-in claim failing for that consumer, not a cosmetic gap. Only _el_fn_complete is declared in histedit.h, but export is export: the symbol table is the contract, not the header.\n\nwcsdup is its own problem and belongs with the rule correction rather than here. src/wcsdup.c:14 is `#ifndef HAVE_WCSDUP` and :16 is `#include \"config.h\"`, so the guard is evaluated before the macro exists, is always true, and the bundled wcsdup always compiles — overriding glibc's for anything that links libedit. Exporting ours therefore reproduces an override the C does by accident. Decide that deliberately rather than by symmetry; note Debian's libedit IMPORTS wcsdup@GLIBC_2.2.5 rather than exporting it, so the deployed target and the oracle already disagree.\n\nTwo version-skew data points from the same run, which is exactly why the oracle is the in-tree C: the oracle and the port both export rl_kill_full_line and Debian does not, and Debian imports wcsdup where the oracle exports it.\n\nReadline coverage needs no work: port 149, Debian's libedit 148, oracle 149, against libreadline.so.8's 736. No readline symbol Debian's libedit exports is missing from the port.\n\nRe-run conformance/abi-shape.sh after this lands; it is the acceptance test and it currently FAILs."
                    }
                )
            }

            {
                id abi-file-surface
                title "Argue and build the FILE * surface, which blocks H_SAVE_FP, EL_SETFP and foreign streams"
                log (
                    {
                        at 2026-08-05T10:58:09.978302682Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "This is one wall behind four separate symptoms, which is why it is its own node rather than a footnote on each.\n\nMeasured by the first conformance run: H_SAVE_FP and H_NSAVE_FP return -1 with \"can't write history\" and write ZERO bytes, where the oracle writes 322 under C.UTF-8 and 306 under C. Four of the six divergences in a 247-operation trace are this. The other two are abi-narrow.\n\nThe same wall also blocks:\n- EL_SETFP, which has no escape hatch at all.\n- el_init with caller-supplied FILE * streams; the ABI crate currently assumes fds 0/1/2, so an application handing us its own streams gets the wrong descriptors.\n- rl_initialize's tcgetattr ECHO test, which is the one divergence in the whole register that fails OPEN — we edit on non-echoing input where the C would not.\n\nCause: CFile is an opaque FILE * and dec:libedit:no-c-ffi bars fputs, ftell and fileno through it. nshedit::history::history_save_fp (crates/nshedit/src/history.rs:1608-1625) documents the hole deliberately, and el.rs:442-458 has `fn fileno(_stream: CFile) -> i32` as a stub.\n\nTHE DECISION COMES FIRST, and no-c-ffi says where. Its enumeration is closed and names this surface as a CANDIDATE third site, not an approved one: \"It passes the test on its face, since a caller's FILE * is opaque to Rust and sem:el.el-init-fn is specified in terms of fileno(fin), but dec:libedit:platform-layer defers it and the enumeration above stays closed until it is argued here.\" So amend no-c-ffi or find a route that does not need the exception — do not just add the extern and move on.\n\nThe two-part test any argument has to carry, from that decision: no pure-Rust route delivers the facility, AND a rule in the corpus specifies behaviour that cannot be implemented without it. Both halves. Note the current failure mode is defensible — \"can't write history\" is an error the C can itself produce — so the honest framing is that we return a plausible error instead of doing the work, which a differential exposes and a unit test would not have.\n\nWorth checking before assuming the exception is needed: whether the caller's FILE * can be reached through its file descriptor alone once fileno exists, since std::fs::File::from_raw_fd would then carry the write without naming any other libc symbol. That would make this one symbol rather than a family, and buffering interleaving between the C's FILE and our writes becomes the question to answer instead.\n\nAcceptance: the four H_SAVE_FP / H_NSAVE_FP lines in conformance/differential.sh stop diverging."
                    }
                )
            }

            {
                id abi-soname
                title "Give the cdylib a SONAME and the install symlinks a consumer's DT_NEEDED actually records"
            }
        )
        log (
            {
                at 2026-08-05T08:39:16.111071698Z
                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                kind @note
                text "The vis/unvis C surface must NOT be exported. This corrects a conclusion reached during abi-errno, which held that \"the real libedit.so on Linux compiles vis.c/unvis.c and exports the symbols\" and proposed a plan node to export them. Measured on this host, the opposite is true.\n\n`nm -D /usr/lib/x86_64-linux-gnu/libedit.so.2` defines 205 symbols, none of them vis-related, and carries three UNDEFINED imports:\n\n    U strunvis@LIBBSD_0.0\n    U strvis@LIBBSD_0.0\n    U vis@LIBBSD_0.0\n\nSo Debian's libedit gets vis from libbsd. `src/Makefile.in:97-98` gates the sources on `@HAVE_VIS_FALSE@`/`@HAVE_UNVIS_FALSE@`, and with libbsd present the configure probe succeeds, so vis.c and unvis.c are never compiled in. glibc itself has no strvis — its only 'vis' symbols are madvise/fadvise relatives.\n\nTwo consequences:\n\n1. No plan node is needed for exporting the vis family, and none should be added. The drop-in contract is what libedit.so.2 actually exports, which excludes them.\n\n2. Exporting them anyway would be actively harmful, not merely redundant. Our artifact is installed with libedit.so.0 symlinked onto it. A process that loads both it and libbsd would then have two definitions of strvis in scope, and ELF interposition binds by load order, so libbsd's own callers could silently start calling ours. That is the same interposition hazard ERR-readline-53 already records for `completion_matches`, arriving from the other direction.\n\nWhat remains true from the abi-errno finding: the core's vis/unvis engine records ENOSPC/ENOMEM/EINVAL faithfully, and no C caller can observe those through us because we export no entry point that would. That is correct rather than a gap — the values are observable only through our own history-file path, which is an internal caller.\n\nOne thing this does raise, and it is NOT resolved: our vis/unvis is translated from NetBSD's vis.c, but the history files a Debian user already has on disk were written by libedit calling *libbsd's* vis. If those two disagree on any encoding, we misread existing history files. libbsd's vis derives from NetBSD's so they are probably identical, but \"probably\" is not a basis for a format we must read. Worth a differential test against libbsd before the history file format is declared done."
            }
        )
    }

