            {
                id spec-corrections-2
                title "Correct the rules the ABI pass and the first conformance run proved wrong"
                log (
                    {
                        at 2026-08-05T10:58:32.379154494Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "Accumulated since spec-rule-corrections closed. Each was found by doing something rather than by re-reading, which is why they were not in the first pass. Verify each against the C before editing, as before — two of these are second-hand.\n\n1. sem:histedit.wcsdup-fn is wrong about the C. VERIFIED BY ME, not second-hand. docs/spec/port/src/histedit.md:1635-1637 says the bundled implementation \"is only compiled in that case\", i.e. when the system lacks wcsdup. It always compiles: src/wcsdup.c:14 is `#ifndef HAVE_WCSDUP` and :16 is `#include \"config.h\"`, so the guard is evaluated before the macro is defined and is unconditionally true. config.h.in:196 has the macro and src/histedit.h:323 guards the DECLARATION with it correctly, so header and source disagree — the declaration disappears on a system that has wcsdup while the definition stays. Consequence: libedit's own wcsdup overrides glibc's for everything that links it. Found by building the oracle.\n\n2. sem:histedit.el-wset-fn and sem:histedit.el-wget-fn give the four prompt arms' argument type as el_wpfunc_t, a type the C never declares. Grep finds it only in a comment at histedit.h:152, which spells a pair of names — el_pfunct_t / el_wpfunct_t — that nothing declares either. src/prompt.h:44 declares exactly one prompt typedef, el_pfunc_t, and src/el.c uses it in all four arms (lines 240, 262, 471, 477). The rule invented a wide/narrow pair by analogy; prompts have no narrow variant because the prompt is stored wide either way. Check whether the same false analogy reached any other rule describing a narrow entry point. Already fixed in the code comments at crates/nshedit-abi/src/histedit.rs; the rules still carry it.\n\n3. sem:histedit.el-wset-fn's EL_TERMINAL arm says it \"Returns 0 on success, -1 if the display arrays could not be allocated\". It also returns -1 whenever the capability lookup failed, however usable the dumb-terminal fallback is. That is ERR-terminal-22, registered against sem:terminal.terminal-set-fn but not against this rule.\n\n4. ERR-readline-35 asserts rl_point and rl_end are byte offsets without qualification. True only of _rl_update_pos, which computes from el_line, the narrow LineInfo. fn_complete2 declares `const LineInfoW *li` and computes over wchar_t, so the copy a completion callback sees is a CHARACTER offset. Qualify the sentence and cross-reference ERR-completion-13, which already records the wide producer. This is a correction, not a new entry — an earlier proposal to add one was withdrawn as a duplicate.\n\n5. crates/nshedit/src/map.rs:1101-1103 carries a comment repeating the map_init_nls claim that spec-rule-corrections corrected away (\"they survive only as the ESC-prefixed keymacros map_init_meta just made\"). The code is right — it queries iswprint — only the prose is stale. Code comment, not a rule, but it belongs in the same sweep.\n\nNot a rule error but the same class of problem, worth deciding here: histedit.h documents H_NEXT_EVDATA, H_DELDATA and H_REPLACE in terms of histdata_t, a type it never declares — it exists only in editline/readline.h:68. A consumer including only histedit.h cannot name it. Possibly inside ERR-core-api-34's annotation-error group; unconfirmed."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-05T11:15:00.248743756Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Todo → Doing (nplan start)"
                    }
                )
            }

            {
                id errata-reconcile-2
                title "Reconcile the register against the ABI pass, the first conformance run and the withdrawals"
                deps (spec-corrections-2)
                log (
                    {
                        at 2026-08-05T10:58:55.169931851Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "Second reconciliation pass. The first one was accurate when written; these went stale as work landed, plus new entries and three withdrawals.\n\nSTALE STATUS LINES — the entry point they describe is no longer a core_gap panic, as of commit e7ddcd7 (abi-visibility):\n- ERR-modes-27 and ERR-core-api-07 (map_bind's argc overwrite, el_wset's collection loop). Now defined as \"always terminate\" via a list_args helper whose returned length is the C's i. BOTH halves of the C's over-read are now unreachable rather than reproduced: a Vec ends where it ends, and el_wset's fixed arity means there is no caller argument list to run off.\n- ERR-core-api-08 — the EL_EDITOR and EL_WORDCHARS halves now reject NULL with -1. Only the EL_SETFP half stays open, blocked on abi-file-surface.\n- ERR-core-api-14 — now observable through el_wget and reproduced end to end.\n- ERR-core-api-16 — reachable now.\n- The summary paragraph at roughly line 113.\n\nSTALE, AND DISPROVEN BY EXECUTION rather than by reading:\n- ERR-history-17's status (docs/errata.md:1301) says \"the ABI H_FUNC opcode is still a core_gap(...) panic ... so no C caller reaches it yet\". It is not. The first conformance run drives H_FUNC through the port's fixed-arity history_w with all eleven varargs and it matches the oracle exactly, including reproducing the defect itself (callbacks receive something other than the installed ref, is-installed-ref=0 on both sides).\n\nNEW ENTRIES:\n- MB_CUR_MAX divergence, documented on the new el::mb_cur_max. The C's follows setlocale; the port's follows the environment, because crate::locale cannot call setlocale and records why. Observable across the ABI: a C application that never calls setlocale under a UTF-8 LANG gets NARROW_HISTORY cleared by el_wset(EL_HIST) where the port leaves it set. Rule: sem:histedit.el-wset-fn's EL_HIST arm. Reach: any C consumer that skips setlocale, common in small programs.\n- rl_terminal_name is written back as a borrowed pointer — the caller's own buffer, getenv(\"TERM\")'s environment string, or the literal \"dumb\" — under a declared type of char * that invites a write, making two of the three cases undefined and free() a corruption. Same call site passes char ** where el_get reads const char **.\n- Seventeen further inert exports beyond ERR-readline-54's list. Four of them default to 0 where GNU's default to 1, so an untouched consumer sees different behaviour before doing anything.\n- rl_already_prompted's direction is reversed relative to GNU: there the application sets it to suppress a redisplay, here libedit writes it and reads it nowhere, so a consumer using the GNU idiom gets a duplicated prompt. Nothing clears it in callback mode.\n- emacs_meta_keymap is defined twice in one translation unit (readline.c:93 and :137). Both tentative, so C merges them and nothing observable changes — but a declaration-by-declaration translation emits two definitions and fails to link.\n- src/wcsdup.c's always-true guard (see spec-corrections-2 item 1). A C defect in no entry under any id.\n\nWITHDRAWALS — proposed during readline-globals-rules, then found to be duplicates when the WHOLE register was checked rather than its readline section. Do not re-add:\n- The rl_point/rl_end unit disagreement is ERR-completion-13, cited by id at the very site that produces it.\n- The completion-append-character condition is ERR-completion-14.\n- The getc(stdin) half of the query-items entry is ERR-completion-12; only the (size_t) cast remains uncovered.\n- ERR-readline-61 narrows to the ownership contract alone; the leak half is ERR-readline-18.\n\nNOT errata, and must not become entries: the six divergences the first conformance run found are port-side gaps, not C defects. The register is C defects only. The narrow-instantiation aborts belong to abi-narrow and the H_SAVE_FP zero-byte writes to abi-file-surface."
                    }
                )
            }
        )
        log (
            {
                at 2026-08-05T08:46:11.185224139Z
                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                kind @note
                text "An eleventh wrong rule, found by abi-callback-types after spec-rule-corrections had already committed. It needs a follow-up pass on that leaf.\n\nsem:histedit.el-wset-fn (histedit.md:913, 919) and sem:histedit.el-wget-fn (histedit.md:615, 618) give the argument type for the EL_PROMPT / EL_RPROMPT / EL_PROMPT_ESC / EL_RPROMPT_ESC arms as el_wpfunc_t. No such type exists in the C. Grepping src/ for el_wpfunc finds it only inside a comment at histedit.h:152, and that comment spells the pair el_pfunct_t / el_wpfunct_t, names nothing declares either. src/prompt.h:44 declares exactly one prompt typedef, el_pfunc_t, and src/el.c uses it in all four prompt arms of el_wset and el_wget (lines 240, 262, 471, 477) and on the narrow eln.c path.\n\nThe four bullets should read el_pfunc_t and el_pfunc_t pointer.\n\nHarmless in practice: the shape is identical, so it changed nothing in the implementation and no Rust is invalidated. The ten def rules for the callback typedefs themselves all match the C byte for byte. This is only the prose in the two el_wset/el_wget rules.\n\nWorth noting for whoever picks this up: the error is that the rule invented a wide/narrow pair by analogy. Prompt callbacks have no narrow variant, because the prompt is stored wide either way. Check whether the same false analogy reached any other rule describing a narrow entry point."
            }
        )
    }

